[
  {
    "_comment": "The library of physical surfaces the world is made of. A biome is not a texture here - it is a handful of these mixed by weight, tinted, and pushed about by moisture, height, slope and macro noise (see content/config/biomes.json). That is the whole reason there are twenty-six of these and not one for each of the eighteen biomes: a hundred and eighty unique paintings is a memory bill and a style that drifts, and it still would not cover the ground between two biomes.\n\nEach entry says where the picture came from, how big a piece of the world it is a picture of, and how the renderer is allowed to bend it. `source` is what the downloader (tools/fetch_terrain.py) needs and what the licence audit reads; everything after it is look, and look is meant to be moved without touching a shader.\n\nWhat the numbers mean:\n  world_scale          metres of ground one turn of the texture covers. Start it near the asset's own physical size - the downloader records that in source.json - or the ground reads at the wrong scale and no amount of tinting fixes it.\n  tint                 multiplied into the albedo. A biome tints on top of this.\n  saturation           pulled towards grey. Poly Haven scans are photographs and photographs are more colourful than a game world can afford beside its own units.\n  normal_strength      how far the surface is allowed to tilt the light. Terrain is the quietest thing in the picture on purpose.\n  ao_strength          how much of the baked occlusion is used.\n  roughness_multiplier scales the roughness channel.\n  height_strength      how hard this material's own relief competes for the surface where two materials meet. Sand fills the hollows between stones because stone's height wins on the high ground and loses in the dips.\n  slope_preference     nought lies flat, one clings to steep ground. What brings rock out on a hillside without anybody painting it there.\n  moisture_response    how much being wet darkens it and drops its roughness.\n  snow_compatibility   how readily snow settles on it. Steep rock keeps less than flat moss.",
    "id": "grass_lush",
    "group": "grass/lush",
    "source": {
      "site": "polyhaven",
      "asset_id": "leafy_grass",
      "license": "CC0",
      "attribution_required": false
